#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134666} :activity/id #uuid "c533ceb7-ea46-55fe-9613-b36245be3181" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893 :topic/id #uuid "c9e24fe5-ad61-5359-b308-a72d2643089c" :topic/math-academy-id 2122} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135046 :tutorial/content "The **orthogonal projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto a span $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is the vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ that is \"closest\" to $\\mathbf{x}.$ Let's work an example to clarify what we mean by \"closest.\"\n\nConsider the following two vectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nGeometrically, $\\mathbf{x}$ can be written as the sum of a vector $k{\\mathbf{a}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and \nanother vector $\\mathbf{z}$ that belongs to the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, as illustrated in the picture below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4754bf048aa6c05cd7ef574af306150c30d435f90afec36f492ed4b8013e899.png)\n\nThe vector $k\\mathbf a$ is called the **orthogonal projection** of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as the sum\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = k \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{z},\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and ${\\mathbf{z}} \\perp \\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nTo compute the orthogonal projection $k \\mathbf{a},$ we can first find the dot product of both sides of the equation above with $\\mathbf{a}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot a & =(ka+z)\\cdot a \\\\ x\\cdot a & =k(a\\cdot a)+z\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{z},$ we have $\\mathbf{z} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 0.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot a & =k(a\\cdot a) \\\\ k & =\\frac{x\\cdot a}{a\\cdot a} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-6)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 6}{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is a shortcut! Instead of going through this process each time, we can compute the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}\\,a\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c57e0ac-99f7-5b01-ace0-7afa2449f0af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3622 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "102b1c5a-b877-5357-9263-23e920a24266" :step/math-academy-id 20237 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133783 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133592 :question/id #uuid "1ad2672a-5773-5f2e-ab81-64e51ab4a7e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5570"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f260476d-35d0-504a-9e0a-86f7fb0049bd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050394}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394731}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "b35849c7-639b-5885-a390-1bb6cfee6fb9" :step/math-academy-id 20238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135047 :tutorial/content "We define the **distance** between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ to be\n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle** $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ to be the acute angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a2670a6df46d3cac249b5c84b960c3877542be0dc656ad64840ef42ce025c4.png)\n\nFinally, we now can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\mathbf{y}} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\mathbf{z}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $\\mathbf{a},$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $\\mathbf{a}.$**" :tutorial/id #uuid "05384c2f-78e1-513a-a296-5a9a856a6652" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3623 :tutorial/title "The Distance  Between a Vector and a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "7e646647-d2d1-5d2a-9a95-42a1ab8c82db" :step/math-academy-id 20239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133593 :question/id #uuid "2031cdd7-c43e-509c-8bb2-4d7362914e7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5571"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144d1258-f8d5-55e9-be38-ec25b6f84bd3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398431}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance  Between a Vector and a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "01e8aa19-ae85-5957-bee9-f3c3292381c1" :step/math-academy-id 20240 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133594 :question/id #uuid "5168f07e-4a22-5e0c-a612-e5c6f77f99eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5572"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8d8c7fb-e69a-5b52-9566-4d2e0c7e54fc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394140}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector onto the Solution Space of a System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b86ddd36-bd23-56a6-b22f-b78dbd8d1d6b" :step/math-academy-id 20241}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto One-Dimensional Subspaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134001}]}
